Trisomy 21 or Down Syndrome (DS) (OMIM 190685) occurs at a frequency of approximately 1/700 live births. In contrast to the variable phenotypic penetrance in many organs, mental retardation (MR) and early onset Alzheimer disease (AD) are invariably present in brains of all DS patients such that DS is the most common genetic cause of mental retardation (Epstein). Changes in the neuropathology, neurochemistry, neurophysiology, and neuropharmacology of DS patient brains indicate that there is abnormal development and maintenance of CNS structure and function. DS is thus a model for mental retardation with abnormal development and neurodegeneration or premature aging. Importantly, two of the genes known to be involved in neurodegenerative disorders, APP and SOD1, map to human chromosome 21 (HC21). To understand fully the molecular
